## Background

`-v` already exists. It is a counted global flag, but every reader of it treats it as a switch, so `-vv` today means exactly what `-v` means. This bead turns the count into the three verbosity levels that decision M4-D17 fixes, without changing anything any existing caller sees:

| Level | Flag | stdout | stderr |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 (default) | none | token-frugal: ids, names, versions and counts | warnings, errors, `info` log events, progress |
| 1 | `-v` | adds the detail fields (paths, evidence, every list item), exactly as `-v` does today | adds `debug` log events, exactly as today |
| 2 | `-vv` | identical to level 1, byte for byte | adds `trace` log events: the release memo's per-request decisions and timings, discovery's route decisions, and waits for the global lock |

Level 2 exists for an agent (or a person) debugging a slow or surprising run: today the only way to learn why `update` fetched instead of answering from the memo, why a client was found under one Wine prefix and not another, or why a command sat for four seconds before doing anything, is to read the code. With `-vv` the binary says so, live, on stderr, without a rebuild.

