### 4. `src/output/trace.rs`: the live trace channel

A new module, declared `pub mod trace;` in `src/output/mod.rs`. It gives deep code (the release memo, discovery, the global lock) one way to report a decision as it makes it, through the run's one stderr reporter:

```rust
//! `trace` log events (`-vv`, M4-D17): the release memo, discovery and the global lock
//! report each decision as they make it, through the run's one stderr reporter, without
//! a handle threaded through every caller. Off unless the run asked for `-vv`; then a
//! site's message is formatted only when it will be written.

static ON: AtomicBool = AtomicBool::new(false);
static SINK: Mutex<Option<SharedReporter>> = Mutex::new(None);

/// Sends trace events to `reporter` for the rest of the process when `cfg` asks for them
/// (`cfg.style.trace()` and not `--quiet`); otherwise turns them off. Replaces any earlier
/// sink.
pub fn install(reporter: &SharedReporter, cfg: &OutputConfig);

/// Whether a trace event would be written now (on this thread, in tests): a site that
/// must do extra work only to describe a decision (read the lock's holder) checks it.
pub fn enabled() -> bool;

/// Writes `message()` as a `trace` log event when tracing is on. `message` is not
/// called otherwise, so a site pays one atomic load when tracing is off.
pub fn emit(message: impl FnOnce() -> String);

/// Test only: runs `f` with this thread's trace events collected instead of written,
/// and returns them in order. Collected whether or not a sink is installed.
#[cfg(test)]
pub(crate) fn capture<T>(f: impl FnOnce() -> T) -> (T, Vec<String>);
```

Implementation rules:

- `emit` checks, in order: a test capture on this thread (push and return); then `ON` (`Ordering::Acquire`; return when off); then clones the `Arc` out of `SINK` (poison-tolerant, `unwrap_or_else(PoisonError::into_inner)`), **releases `SINK`**, and only then calls `output::lock(&reporter).log(LogLevel::Trace, &message())`. No lock is ever held while another is taken, so tracing cannot deadlock against `ProgressSink` (which holds its phase map while it locks the reporter).
- `Reporter::log` applies its own gate as well, so even a mis-installed sink never prints a trace below level 2 or under `--quiet`.
- The capture is a `thread_local!` `RefCell<Option<Vec<String>>>`, reset by a drop guard, modelled on the failpoint override (`src/fsx/failpoint.rs:214-264`). It lets each site's unit tests assert exact lines without racing other tests in the same process.
- **Every `emit` call happens on the thread that called the site's public function**, never inside a `run_bounded` closure: a worker measures and returns what it learned, and the caller emits. That keeps the test capture reliable and the order of lines deterministic.

Decided while writing this bead: a process-wide sink installed once per run, rather than a handle in `Context`. Rejected: (i) a `TraceSink` in `Context` passed down to every site: `GlobalLock::acquire` alone has about 20 production callers, many in hot-spot files that D2 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.4.2`, `--if-match` on `install`, `update`, `sync` and `copy`), D8 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.4.8`, The approval checked again under the commit lock), E5 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.5.5`, The repair transaction) and G3 (`omad-7ccp`, Group commit, derived writes and deferred directory fsyncs) rewrite, so the plumbing would be a large cross-cutting diff for a size-S leaf; (ii) collecting trace lines into each outcome's `notes` and printing them after the command, as `-v` does: a lock wait or a slow fetch would be reported only once it ended, too late for an agent watching a stuck run, and every command's outcome would need a new field. Precedent for process-wide run state: `PANIC_STATE` in `src/cli.rs:1244`, and the statics in `src/output/mod.rs:58-60`.

