### 6. Site 1: the release memo (`src/resolve/cache.rs`)

`<key>` below is `memo::key(&w.r#ref, w.channel)`, the memo's own key (`gh:o/foo|stable`). Ages are whole seconds since `entry.fetched_at`, 0 when it lies in the future. Timings are whole milliseconds from `std::time::Instant`.

**Per wish, in `get()`'s decision loop** (`:238-271`), one line. Format the decision lines with a new pure function, for example `fn decision_line(w: &Wanted, transient: bool, entry: Option<&MemoEntry>, policy: Freshness, decision: Decision, now: Timestamp) -> String` (whether the wish is memoized follows from `transient` and the ref, as `memoized()` decides it), so the wording is unit-tested without a cache; the "answered earlier in this run" line is formatted where the run map is read:

| Case | Message |
|---|---|
| answered earlier in this run (`run.get(w)` hit) | `release memo <key>: answered earlier in this run (<origin>)`, `<origin>` the `Origin` wire name (`memo`, `probed`, `fetched`, `stale_fallback`) |
| `Answer(Memo)` | `release memo <key>: fresh, answered from the memo (fetched <age> s ago, window <window> s); no request` |
| `Answer(StaleFallback)` | `release memo <key>: offline, answered from the memo whatever its age (fetched <age> s ago)` |
| `Probe` under `Window` | `release memo <key>: stale (fetched <age> s ago), probing the source` |
| `Probe` under `Revalidate` | `release memo <key>: revalidating (fetched <age> s ago), probing the source` |
| `Fetch`, the entry holds a failure | `release memo <key>: remembered a failure (fetched <age> s ago), fetching` |
| `Fetch`, a set but no probe token | `release memo <key>: stale, and the source offers no probe (fetched <age> s ago); fetching` |
| `Fetch` under `Refresh` | `release memo <key>: --refresh, fetching` |
| `Fetch` under `OfflineOnly`, nothing remembered | `release memo <key>: offline and not remembered; the fetch is refused offline` |
| `Fetch`, nothing remembered | `release memo <key>: not remembered, fetching` |
| not memoized: a pinned version | `release memo <key>: version <v> pinned, not memoized; fetching` |
| not memoized: a transient cache (no memo file) | `release memo <key>: no memo for this command; fetching` |
| not memoized: a `local` or `url` ref | `release memo (local ref): not memoized; read now` or `release memo (url ref): …` |

**Per probe, in `probe()`:** the `run_bounded` closure also returns `started.elapsed()`; the loop over `probed` (caller thread) emits one line per wish:

- `release probe <key>: token unchanged, kept the remembered set (<ms> ms)`;
- `release probe <key>: failed (<code>), answered with the failure (<ms> ms)`, `<code>` the error's `code.wire()`;
- `release probe <key>: token changed, fetching (<ms> ms)`, or `release probe <key>: no probe answer, fetching (<ms> ms)` when the source returned none.

**Per fetch batch, in `get()`:** time `fetch_once_reporting` and emit `release fetch: <n refs> from <k sources> in <ms> ms`, the counts written with `output::count` (`1 ref from 1 source`, `3 refs from 2 sources`; `<k>` the distinct `SourceId`s among them), then one line per result: `release fetch <key>: newest <version> (<m> releases), remembered` (or `…, not remembered` when `MemoEntry::of` gives `None`), `release fetch <key>: no release in this channel`, or `release fetch <key>: failed (<code>), remembered|not remembered`. Local and url refs use `(local ref)` or `(url ref)` in place of the key here too.

The probe and fetch timings are what M4-D17 calls "per-request … timings": a probe is one request, and a fetch batch is the requests each source makes for its wishes.

D5 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.4.5`, `omaddons apply`) later makes every command use `Freshness::Revalidate` under `apply`; the `revalidating` line above already covers it. If D5 has landed when this bead is worked, check that its `force_revalidate` choice reaches `get()` as `Freshness::Revalidate` and is traced as such; if it added a new decision path, give it a line too.

### 7. Site 2: discovery (`src/discover/mod.rs`, `src/discover/cache.rs`)

`<route>` is the route's `DiscoverySource` kind as it serializes (`product_db`, `launcher <name>`, `process`, `extra_root`, `scan`, `manual`, `registered`, `remembered`), followed by ` (<qualifier>)` when the qualifier is not empty; build it with a small `fn route_label(route: &Route) -> String` beside `Route` (`src/discover/mod.rs:446-462`). `<root>` is the root's path as discovery holds it (`path.display()`): an install root, never a path below a client's `WTF/`.

- **`Candidates::add`** (`src/discover/mod.rs:575-624`): when it keeps a new root, `discovery route <route>: <root>, first route to reach it`; when a later route reaches a kept root, `discovery route <route>: <root>, already reached by <first route>; hints merged`. A root that cannot be canonicalized stays a `debug` diagnostic only, as today.
- **`discover_cached`** (`src/discover/cache.rs:220-275`): per root request, `discovery root <root>: reused from the discovery cache` at the reuse branch (`:237`) and `discovery root <root>: parsed` at the parse branch (`:250`). The `is gone` and `is not a WoW root` cases stay `debug` diagnostics.

So `status -vv` shows the routes in order (product.db, launcher configs, running processes, extra roots, the scan, registered roots, remembered roots, vision §5.2), and `status --cached -vv`, which runs no route, shows only the reuse lines.

Decided while writing this bead: the per-root reuse-or-parse lines in `src/discover/cache.rs` are part of the discovery site. The plan names only `discover/mod.rs` for "discovery route decisions", but `collect()` is called from `discover_cached`, and whether a root was reused or parsed is the other half of why a discovery was slow.

### 8. Site 3: the global lock (`src/fsx/lock.rs`)

In `GlobalLock::acquire_with_timeout` (`:79-122`), on the caller's thread:

| When | Message |
|---|---|
| `try_lock` succeeds at the first try | `global lock: acquired at once` |
| the first `WouldBlock` | `global lock: held by omaddons <command> (pid <pid>, since <started_at>); waiting up to <timeout> ms`, from `read_holder`; `global lock: held by another omaddons process; waiting up to <timeout> ms` when the holder info is unreadable |
| acquired after waiting | `global lock: acquired after <ms> ms (<n> retries)` |
| the timeout | `global lock: not acquired within <timeout> ms`, then the `locked` error exactly as today |

`read_holder` runs only when `trace::enabled()`, so an untraced run does no extra read. `InflightLock` is not traced. The holder's `command` is an omaddons command path and `started_at` an RFC 3339 UTC instant; neither is private.

This is the first `crate::output` import in `src/fsx/`. It is allowed: the call writes to stderr through the reporter, never to the filesystem, so the one-writer rule (`tests/single_writer.rs`) is unaffected.

### 9. What the trace messages may and may not say

- **Privacy (common rule 9):** a trace message may name paths below `<state>` and `<cache>`, client install roots, refs, versions, error codes and omaddons command names. It never names a path below a `WTF/`, and never an account, realm or character. A `local` or `url` ref is named by its kind only, because a local path or a URL can carry the user's directory names or a signed query string. Decided while writing this bead: the kind-only rule for `local` and `url` refs, as the conservative reading of "never below `WTF/`".
- **Wording is not contract.** The `level` value `trace` is contract; a trace `message` is free text that may change in any release, like a `debug` message. README says so, so nobody parses it.
- **Bounded (common rule 15):** every line belongs to a unit of work that is already bounded: a wish (at most the command's own refs, and the memo holds at most `RELEASE_MEMO_MAX_ENTRIES`), a root (bounded by `ScanLimits`), a lock acquisition (one or two per transaction). Decided while writing this bead: no cap on trace lines and no new constant, because a line exists only where bounded work happens and the user asked for every line.
- **stdout never changes:** nothing reads `Style::trace()` while rendering stdout, and the source scan above keeps it that way.
- Decided while writing this bead: the wording of every message in sections 6 to 8, the dimmed human label `trace:`, and that `-vvv` and more act as `-vv`. The plan fixes the three sites, the level and its wire name, not the text; the close comment confirms or adjusts the wording.

