### The decisions that shape it

- **M4-D53: `status` issues gain two keys, and the old fixes point at the doctor.** The part this bead implements: "`error::internal_fix_for` returns `omaddons doctor` for every command but `status` and `doctor` themselves: `status` → `status --cached`, as today; `doctor` → `omaddons doctor health`. This retires DEC-5. `store::status_fix` points at `omaddons doctor explain fm-schemas-state-corrupt`." Why: the vision (§5.9: "internal errors use `omaddons doctor`"); the manual states' current advice is false. The other half (F8's): `status.targets[].issues[]` and `data.issues[]` gain `auto_fixable` and `explain`; an auto-fixable finding's remediation becomes `omaddons doctor fix --only <fm> [-t <id>]`; `status --check` also fails on P0/P1 issues. Rejected: a new status field per finding kind (one `issues[]` list is the shape the widget and agents already read).
- **M4-D1:** the handoff "the `internal` and `state_corrupt` fixes pointing at the doctor" is in M4's scope, because it is cheap only while the code it touches is fresh.
- **M4-D40 (context):** in the doctor tree an internal error exits 74 (`io_error`), never 1 or 101. A fix that sent a failing `doctor` command to `omaddons doctor` would rerun the failing tree; `doctor health` runs only the `cached` and `quick` tiers and creates no run directory.
- **M4-D29 (context):** `doctor health` and `status` run the same `cached` and `quick` detectors; `doctor` runs every tier but `deep` and isolates each detector (`catch_unwind`, a `detector-crashed` finding), which is why it is the right next step after a bug elsewhere.
- **M4-D30 (context):** `doctor explain` takes a failure-mode id (all its live instances) and `-t` narrows to one target, named by its id; instance keys use the target's key.
- **DEC-4 and M4-D55 (context):** no wire name is added, renamed or removed. A fix's text and argv are values, not names, but agents run them, so the close comment lists every changed fix with a before → after note (common rule 6).
- **§8.1 default 1:** prefer an existing code, action or key; this bead adds none.

**How it serves the vision.** G4, agent ergonomics ("the first command an agent guesses works"): an agent follows `fix_argv`; after this bead that argv always runs and always leads somewhere that explains the state, instead of to a command that repeats the failure. G7 ("`doctor` follows the world-class doctor contract"): every error that a broken home causes names the doctor as the place to look. The thesis that omaddons stays the sole, safe, undoable authority over what it manages: when omaddons cannot finish something itself, its own error says exactly which of its commands explains what is at stake, rather than sending the user to hand edits.

## Technical Approach

### 1. The internal fix (`src/error.rs:357-379`)

```rust
/// The default fix of an `internal` error: the doctor (M4-D53, which retires DEC-5).
pub fn internal_fix() -> Suggestion {
    Suggestion::command(["omaddons", "doctor"])
}

/// What an internal error says when no command is left to suggest.
const BUG_REPORT: &str =
    "this is a bug in omaddons: report the message above with the output of omaddons --version";

/// The fix for an internal error raised while `command` ran with `argv`, never the
/// failing command itself (M4-D53):
/// - `status` → `status --cached` (no discovery, no network); `status --cached` → a report;
/// - `doctor health` → a report; every other `doctor` path → `doctor health` (the cached
///   and quick tiers, no run directory);
/// - any other command, and a panic before the command is known → `omaddons doctor`.
pub fn internal_fix_for(command: &str, argv: &[String]) -> Suggestion {
    match command {
        "status" if !argv.iter().any(|a| a == "--cached") => {
            Suggestion::command(["omaddons", "status", "--cached"])
        }
        "status" | "doctor health" => Suggestion::text(BUG_REPORT),
        c if c == "doctor" || c.starts_with("doctor ") => {
            Suggestion::command(["omaddons", "doctor", "health"])
        }
        _ => internal_fix(),
    }
}
```

- The arms are tried in order: `doctor health` must be matched before the `doctor` prefix arm.
- The `doctor` arm matches the command names `command_name` gives the doctor tree: bare `doctor` (whatever A1's canonical name for it is, `doctor` or `doctor diagnose`), `doctor diagnose`, `doctor explain`, `doctor fix`, `doctor diff`, `doctor undo`, `doctor gc`, `doctor ls`, `doctor capabilities`, `doctor robot-docs`. A name that merely starts with the letters `doctor` and no space (none exists) is not matched.
- Decided while writing this bead: `doctor health`'s own internal error gets the bug-report prose, as `status --cached`'s does. M4-D53 names only `doctor` → `doctor health`; applied to `doctor health` itself that would rerun the failing command, which the plan's acceptance forbids. `status --cached` keeps its prose, because M4-D53 keeps the `status` arm "as today".
- Decided while writing this bead: the prose text is one constant, `BUG_REPORT`, unchanged from today; it names `omaddons --version`, which `PROSE_COMMANDS` admits.
- Following the fixes always ends: `status` → `status --cached` → prose; any `doctor` path → `doctor health` → prose; anything else → `doctor` → `doctor health` → prose. At most three hops, never the same argv twice. A unit test and an end-to-end test hold this for every command path.

