### 2. The trait gains four defaulted methods (`tests/doctor/mod.rs`)

```rust
/// A unique case name; fixtures that share an id return distinct names.
fn name(&self) -> String { self.id().to_owned() }
/// False for a negative fixture: a state that must NOT produce this finding.
fn finds(&self) -> bool { true }
/// The evidence keys this fixture's finding carries (the harness's true-positive property).
fn evidence_keys(&self) -> &'static [&'static str] { &[] }
/// Whether a copy of the corrupted home, restored at the same path, means the same as the original.
fn copy_safe(&self) -> bool { true }
```

Each with a doc comment saying which harness property reads it. Decided while writing this bead: these four, because the properties need a unique case name (F10, `omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.6.10`, registers several fixtures for one id), negative fixtures (a current skill written by another build is not a finding), declared evidence keys (part 2 fills them for every fixture) and a way to opt out of copying (item 6). All defaulted, so every existing fixture and suite compiles unchanged.

### 3. Lanes (`tests/doctor/harness/lane.rs`)

```rust
pub struct Lane { dir: tempfile::TempDir, pub home: Home, template: PathBuf, fixtures: Vec<Box<dyn Fixture>>, names: Vec<String> }
impl Lane {
    pub fn build(log: &TestLog, name: &str) -> Lane;
    pub fn build_many(log: &TestLog, label: &str, names: &[&str]) -> Lane;
    pub fn fixture(&self) -> &dyn Fixture;          // the first
    pub fn fixtures(&self) -> &[Box<dyn Fixture>];
    pub fn restore(&mut self, log: &TestLog);
    pub fn fidelity(&mut self, log: &TestLog) -> Result<(), String>;
}
pub fn copy_tree(from: &Path, to: &Path);
pub fn clear_tree(dir: &Path);
```

- **`build`**: `fixtures::all()`, keep the fixture whose `name()` is `name` (panic listing the names when none is); `Home::at(<dir>/home, &["wow", "wow_classic"])`, the shape F1's and E3's fixtures are written for (retail and Mists Classic in one Battle.net root); `corrupt`; then `copy_tree(<dir>/home, <dir>/template)`. `build_many` corrupts several fixtures in order (the pairs of part 5 use it). The lane directory is a `tempfile::TempDir` (under the system temp dir, which T1's tree checks accept), removed when the lane drops. Lane build time is logged.
- **`restore`**: for a lane whose fixtures are all `copy_safe()`, `clear_tree(<dir>/home)` then `copy_tree(<dir>/template, <dir>/home)`: the home is back at exactly the path it was built at. Otherwise, rebuild: clear, `Home::at` again, fresh fixture instances from `fixtures::all()`, `corrupt` again. Restore time is logged.
- Decided while writing this bead: **restore in place**, because every state file holds absolute paths, so a copy is valid only at the path it was built at; copying to a new path would need every absolute path rewritten in every state file, and journals also record inode identities no copy keeps. Rebuilding from scratch per case is the slow path, used only for fixtures that fail the fidelity test.
- **`copy_tree`** preserves: entry kinds; file bytes; permission bits (a directory's mode applied after its children are copied, so a 0555 directory can be populated; files written owner-writable first, then their mode set); symlink targets (`std::fs::read_link`, `std::os::unix::fs::symlink`, never followed); modification times of files and directories (`File::set_times`, a directory's after its children) and of symlinks (`rustix::fs::utimensat(CWD, path, &Timestamps { .. }, AtFlags::SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW)`), because `ORPHAN_MIN_AGE` and stamps read them. A FIFO, socket or device panics with its path. Identities are not preserved and cannot be.
- **`clear_tree`**: walk and make every directory owner-writable (`0o700` added to its mode), then `std::fs::remove_dir_all`. (A fixture may plant a 0555 `AddOns`.)
- **`fidelity`** (decided while writing this bead): a copy is a different set of inodes, so a fixture whose meaning depends on an identity recorded before the copy (an engine journal's step identities, an flock the test process holds on the original lock file) could mean something else after a restore. `fidelity` diagnoses the original (`run::diagnose`, before any restore), restores, diagnoses the copy, and compares the two `findings` arrays after removing the keys `contract::VOLATILE` declares for `doctor diagnose` and every member named `identity`, `ino` or `born_ns` at any depth. A difference returns `Err("fixture <name> does not survive a copy of its home: return false from copy_safe(), or make its state copy-independent")`, with both arrays logged. Then it restores again, so the first case starts clean.
- Lanes are built and used inside one thread each (fixtures are not `Send`): the thread calls `fixtures::all()` itself.

### 4. Subject maps and bookkeeping (`tests/doctor/harness/subject.rs`)

```rust
pub enum Kind { File, Dir, Symlink }
pub struct Entry { pub kind: Kind, pub mode: u32, pub sha256: Option<String>, pub link: Option<PathBuf>, pub ino: u64, pub born_ns: Option<i128>, pub mtime_ns: i128 }
pub struct SubjectMap(pub BTreeMap<PathBuf, Option<Entry>>);
impl SubjectMap {
    pub fn take(roots: &[PathBuf], keep_out: &Bookkeeping) -> SubjectMap;
    pub fn same_content(&self, other: &SubjectMap) -> Result<(), Vec<String>>;
}
pub struct Bookkeeping { excluded: Vec<PathBuf> }
impl Bookkeeping {
    pub fn base(home: &Home) -> Bookkeeping;
    pub fn with_run(self, home: &Home, run_id: &str, tx: Option<&str>) -> Bookkeeping;
    pub fn check_subject(&self, roots: &[PathBuf]);
}
pub fn root_map(home: &Home, also_excluded: &[PathBuf]) -> BTreeMap<String, common::trees::Entry>;
pub fn leftovers(home: &Home) -> BTreeSet<PathBuf>;
pub fn history_lines(home: &Home) -> Vec<String>;
pub fn lock_identity(home: &Home) -> Option<(u64, Option<i128>)>;
```

- **`SubjectMap::take`**: for each root, the root itself and, for a directory, every entry below it (symlinks never followed), with its kind, permission bits, SHA-256 of a file's bytes, a symlink's target, inode, birth time (`rustix::fs::statx` with `StatxFlags::BTIME`, `None` where the filesystem reports none) and mtime; an absent root is `None`; excluded paths are skipped. Decided while writing this bead (plan against code): the plan names `common::trees::tree_hash` for subject snapshots, but it hashes directories only, and a set-aside's subjects include single files (the journal, the in-flight lock); this map records the same facts for files, directories and absent paths, and keeps identity and mtime for the checks that need them.
- **`same_content`** compares kind, mode, `sha256` and `link`, ignoring identity and mtime; `Err` lists the first `DIFF_PATHS_SHOWN` (T1's constant, 20) differences, each "<path>: <before> → <after>".
- **`Bookkeeping::base`** excludes, by exact path: `<state>/doctor/`, `<state>/net.log`, `<state>/net.log.1`, `<state>/status.json`, `<state>/history.jsonl`, `<cache>/history_tail.json`, `<cache>/backups.json`, `<cache>/discovery.json`, `<cache>/doorbell.json`. **`with_run`** adds `<state>/journal/<tx>.repair.jsonl`, `<state>/inflight/<tx>.lock`, `<meta>/snapshots/<tx>.repair/` for every client dir of the home, and `<cache>/quarantine/<run_id>/`. Nothing is excluded by a directory name alone: this is the subject rule ("only the run's own bookkeeping is excluded, never a fixer's subject"; a set-aside's subject is the original journal file, its in-flight lock file, its stage and both snapshot halves, although `journal/` and `inflight/` are bookkeeping for every other fixer).
- **`check_subject`** panics "the subject rule: <root> is bookkeeping" when a root is excluded or lies inside an excluded path.
- **`root_map`**: `common::trees::tree_entries(home.env.root())` with `<state>/doctor/` and `also_excluded` removed (by key prefix): the purity view over `<state>`, `<config>`, `<cache>`, `HOME`, the Battle.net prefix and every client dir, and the HTTP fixtures.
- **`leftovers`**: every entry whose name starts with `.omaddons-`, without descending into it, under `<state>` (without `doctor/`), `<config>`, `<cache>` (without `quarantine/`) and each client's `<meta>` (without `snapshots/*.repair/`).
- **`history_lines`**: the raw lines of `<state>/history.jsonl` (empty when absent). **`lock_identity`**: the lock file's inode and birth time, `None` while it does not exist.

### 5. Doctor-run helpers (`run.rs`) and the capabilities view (`caps.rs`)

Every command runs through `log.run`/`log.cmd` with `--json --progress json`, every doctor envelope goes through `common::doctor::check_envelope`, and every `Ran` through `assert_private()`.

```rust
pub fn row(id: &str) -> &'static FailureMode;                        // from omaddons::doctor::registry::FAILURE_MODES
pub fn selection_args(fx: &dyn Fixture) -> Vec<String>;
pub fn diagnose(log: &TestLog, home: &Home, fx: &dyn Fixture, extra: &[&str]) -> Ran;
pub fn fix(log: &TestLog, home: &Home, fxs: &[&dyn Fixture], failpoints: Option<&str>) -> (Ran, Option<FixReport>);
pub fn undo(log: &TestLog, home: &Home, run: Option<&str>) -> (Ran, UndoReport);
pub fn status(log: &TestLog, home: &Home, cached: bool, env: &[(String, String)]) -> Ran;
pub fn recover(log: &TestLog, home: &Home, fx: &dyn Fixture) -> Ran;
pub fn repair_line(home: &Home, tx: &str) -> Option<Value>;
pub fn repair_journal(home: &Home) -> Option<JournalAtCrash>;
pub fn actions(path: &Path) -> Vec<Value>;
pub fn target_of(log: &TestLog, home: &Home, key: &str) -> (String, u32);
pub fn caps(log: &TestLog, home: &Home) -> DoctorCaps;
pub fn assert_blast_radius(caps: &DoctorCaps, fixers: &[&str], actions: &[Value], home: &Home);
```

- Decided while writing this bead: the harness reads the **static** `FAILURE_MODES`, never `failure_modes()`, which adds E6's test rows when the test process has `OMADDONS_TEST_FIXERS=1` exported while every child has it removed.
- **`selection_args`**: the fixture's `run_args()` when non-empty (the runtime rows), else `--only <id>`, plus `--include-deep` when the row's tier is `Deep` and `--online` when the row is online. `diagnose` runs `doctor` with those (or with `extra` replacing the selection when `extra` is non-empty), and the fixture's `run_env()`. `fix` runs `doctor fix --only <ids, comma-joined>`, adding `--force --yes` when any row `needs_force`, `--include-deep`/`--online` as above, every fixture's `run_env()`, and `OMADDONS_FAILPOINTS=<failpoints>` when given; it returns `None` for the report when the child died by a signal.
- **`FixReport { exit, run_id, tx: Option<String>, actions_taken, delegated: Vec<Value>, actions_jsonl: Option<PathBuf>, findings_after }`** and **`UndoReport { exit, run_id, reverted_run, tx, undo_tx, actions_taken, already_undone }`** read the doctor envelope's top-level keys.
- **`recover`** runs **the recovering command**: `config set check_interval_hours 6 --json --progress json`, a lock-taking command that recovers both journal families, as §6.15 and the matrices of E5 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.5.5`) and F1 use. One exception, decided while writing this bead (plan against code): a command without discovery treats every client as possibly running, and recovery of a journal that renames `WTF/` with a step refuses while its client runs or may run (`src/engine/journal.rs:1039-1046`), so `config set` only warns about such a journal and never recovers it. For a fixture whose fixer is a delegation and whose planted folder journal has `writes_wtf: true` in its `prepared` line (row 9's restore journal), `recover` runs instead `install gh:harness/recover --file <archive> -t <that journal's target id> --offline --json --progress json`, the discovery-running change F1 uses, with an archive built once per lane by `common::archives::zip_text(&[("Recover/Recover.toc", "## Interface: <interface>\n## Title: Recover\nRecover.lua\n"), ("Recover/Recover.lua", "")])` for the target's interface (`target_of`). Within a lane the command never changes, so its own effect cancels out of every comparison (part 4 computes both references with it).
- **`repair_journal`** returns the single `<state>/journal/*.repair.jsonl` as `JournalAtCrash { tx, lines: Vec<Value>, last: Option<String> /* the last complete line's "type" */, primitives: Vec<Value>, fixes: Vec<Value> }` (a torn last line is ignored, as recovery ignores it); two repair journals at once panic.
- **`target_of`** runs `targets --json` and returns the `id` and `interface` of the target whose `key` is `key`.
- **`caps`** reads `doctor capabilities --json` (`detectors`, `fixers[{id, writes_to, ops, enabled, needs_force}]`, `write_scopes[{scope, path}]`) once E4 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.5.4`) has landed. E4 is not an ancestor of F9, so until then it builds the same view from `FAILURE_MODES` and fsx's scope roots for this home: `State` → `<state>`, `Config` → `<config>`, `Cache` → `<cache>`, `ClientMeta` → each client's `<meta>`, `AddOns` → each client's `Interface/AddOns`, `Skill` → `<skills>` (`$HOME/.claude/skills/omaddons`, under the lane's `HOME`) and each `history_tail.skill_dirs[]` entry. It logs which source it used.
- **`assert_blast_radius`**: every action line's `path` and `to` lie under a root whose scope is in one of `fixers`' `writes_to`; no path has a `WTF` component; no path is `<state>/lock`. A failure names the line, the path and the declared scopes.

### 6. The case pool (`pool.rs`) and constants (`mod.rs`)

- `HARNESS_SLOTS = max(HARNESS_SLOTS_MIN, 2 × std::thread::available_parallelism())`: one counting semaphore (`Mutex` + `Condvar`) shared by every `#[test]` of the binary, so libtest's own parallelism and the lanes' threads never oversubscribe the machine. Decided while writing this bead: no environment variable to tune it (a new harness variable would need an `ENV_VARS` row, and the gate must not depend on a developer's setting).
- `par_lanes<T>(log: &TestLog, lanes: Vec<T>, f: impl Fn(&TestLog, T) + Sync)`: each lane in a scoped thread; `f` runs that lane's cases, each acquiring a slot and logging into `log.case(<lane>/<case>)`; every case is wrapped in `catch_unwind`, so all cases run, and the test then panics once with every failing case's name and transcript path.
- Constants (test-harness constants, so no `src/limits.rs` row, as T1's): `LANES_PER_FIXER = 4`, `CHECKED_SWEEP_MAX = 256`, `RECOVER_SWEEP_MAX = 64`, `LEFTOVER_AGE = Duration::from_secs(2 * 3600)`, `HARNESS_SLOTS_MIN = 4`.

### 7. The self-tests (`tests/doctor/harness/props/selftest.rs`)

1. **`a_restored_lane_equals_its_template`**: a lane built with `build_many` over a synthetic plant written by the test (no fixture): a file 0400, a file 0755, a directory 0555 holding a file, a symlink to a relative target, a file whose mtime is set three hours back, and a `.omaddons-x` temp aged the same; save; then modify the home (write a file, chmod one, delete one); `restore`. Asserts: `tree_entries` of home equals the template's; every mtime (files, the 0555 directory, the symlink) equals the template's to the nanosecond; the 0555 directory is 0555 again; `clear_tree` then removes everything (also as a non-root user, the 0555 directory included). Logs the restore time.
2. **`subject_maps_compare_content_and_name_differences`**: table-driven, one `log.case` per row: `absent_vs_present` (a root absent then created), `mode_change` (0644 → 0600), `same_size_edit` (one byte changed), `symlink_retarget`, `identity_only` (a file replaced by a copy with the same bytes and mode: `same_content` is `Ok`), `mtime_only` (`Ok`), `bookkeeping_by_id` (a `<state>/journal/<tx>.repair.jsonl` of the run's tx is skipped, another tx's is not), `subject_inside_bookkeeping` (a root under `<state>/doctor/` panics with "the subject rule:"; checked with `catch_unwind`). Each asserts the expected `Ok`/`Err` and, for `Err`, that the first difference names the changed path.
3. **`the_pool_runs_every_case_and_reports_every_failure`**: `par_lanes` over three trivial lanes, two of whose cases panic on purpose, inside `catch_unwind`: the aggregate message names both failing cases and both transcript paths, and the third case's log records its completion.
4. **`every_fixture_survives_a_copy_or_says_so`**: for every fixture of `fixtures::all()` except the `fm-test-` ones, a lane and its `fidelity`, in parallel through the pool: a fixture with `copy_safe() == true` must return `Ok`; one with `false` is skipped with a logged note. Then a negative control: the fidelity of F1's `fm-concurrency_primitives-lock-held-long` fixture (its guard holds an flock on the original lock file's inode) is run regardless of its flag and must return `Err`. Wherever this test fails for a fixture, set that fixture's `copy_safe()` to `false` in its file, with a comment saying what a copy cannot carry, and list it in the close comment (expected: the lock-held-long fixture; journal fixtures whose verdict depends on recorded identities, if any).
5. **`blast_radius_flags_a_write_outside_the_declared_scopes`**: `assert_blast_radius` over synthetic action lines on a fresh home (one under `<cache>` for a fixer declaring only `Config`, one under `WTF/`, one at `<state>/lock`): each panics (checked with `catch_unwind`) naming the line; a line under `<state>/doctor/runs/<run>/quarantine/config/` for a fixer declaring `State` passes.

