## Background

H3 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.8.3`, the determinism audit) makes "one command on one unchanged home prints the same bytes, except the declared volatile keys" a gate test over every command path. It has four parts: a registry of the keys that may differ (`contract::VOLATILE`), a repeat of every contract matrix row on two fresh copies of its home, a coverage test that every command path has a success and a failure row, and a source scan that keeps `HashMap` and `HashSet` out of serialized types. It serves G-M4-5 ("repeated runs print identical bytes") and checkpoint criterion 14's determinism part: "Repeated runs of every command path print identical bytes, after stripping only the keys the volatile registry declares."

**This part** completes the registry so that it can be the only thing any comparison masks. It is its own step because the repeat (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.8.3.2`) and the snapshot scrubber both read it, and because its shape changes: the rows other leaves added are migrated in this one commit.

**M4-D18, restated.** Keys whose values legitimately change between runs are declared in `contract::VOLATILE`, each with its reason. The plan names `meta.*`, the doctor's `run_id`, `run_dir`, `started_at` and `finished_at`, stored plans' `plan_id` and `plan_expires_at`, and the existing `duration_ms` and `lock_held_ms`; §6.8 adds `data.**.tx` and `data.**.run` on real runs, `checked_at`-style memo times and the doctor's `summary.duration_ms`. The repeat strips only declared keys, and a stripped key that is not declared fails. Why: the vision put every volatile value in `meta`, but `duration_ms` and `lock_held_ms` already sit in `data`, and the doctor contract puts run identity at the top level; declaring them is honest, and renaming them would be churn. **Rejected:** moving every volatile value into `meta`, which breaks the doctor contract's shapes and the existing snapshots.

**What exists when this part starts** (D4, `omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.4.4`, and E3, `omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.5.3`, both create the file if absent; both are transitive blockers of H3):

- `src/contract/volatile.rs` with `pub const VOLATILE: &[(&str, &str)]` (pattern, reason): `meta.*`, `data.**.duration_ms`, `data.**.lock_held_ms` ("timings"), `data.**.plan_id` and `data.**.plan_expires_at` ("a new plan per previewed change"), and E3's `run_id`, `run_dir`, `started_at`, `finished_at` ("a new doctor run per invocation") and `summary.duration_ms` ("timing"). F leaves that landed may have added rows (F1, `omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.6.1`, and F3, `omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.6.3`: `findings[].evidence.age_s` for `doctor diagnose` and `doctor fix`, `instances[].evidence.age_s` for `doctor explain`). Read the file as it stands and migrate every row.
- `contract::OPAQUE` (A3, `omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.1.3`, schemas, the walk and `OPAQUE`) is the precedent for a registry of declared exceptions: `Opaque { scope, path, source, reason }`, paths in A9's syntax (`a.b`, `a[]` the items of array `a`, `a{}` a map's values), and a test that every row matches a position of the published schemas through `contract::schema::walk` (`Visit::Position { path, node }` and `Visit::Property { … }`, local `$ref`s resolved, in-place branches walked at one path).
- `app::COMMANDS` (A1, `omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.1.1`, per-command facts in `COMMANDS`) holds one row per runnable path, each with `dictionary` (`General` or `Doctor`) and `data: fn(&mut SchemaGenerator) -> Schema`. A3's `contract::schema` has `envelope_schema()` and `command_schema(entry)`; E3's doctor commands publish their report as their `data` schema, with the report keys at the envelope's root.

**Facts this part relies on** (as of `c0ab5e9`): README's "The JSON contract" has `### Fields` at `README.md:1790-1806` and `### Stability` at `README.md:1959-1961`, and no test reads README today. Several M3 tokens put `FileStamp::fingerprint()` (device, inode, size, mtime, change time: `src/fsx/stamp.rs:73-89`) in their `observed` part: `errors`' `dismiss.precondition` (`src/app/errors_status.rs:215-218`) and `errors dismiss`' `precondition` (`src/app/precondition.rs:419-440`). The repeat runs on copies, which have new inodes, so these differ there.

## Technical Approach

### 1. The row type

In `src/contract/volatile.rs`:

```rust
/// Why a key's value differs between two runs that start from the same home.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]
pub enum VolatileKind {
    /// A duration the run measured (`duration_ms`, `lock_held_ms`).
    Timing,
    /// An instant the run read from the wall clock (`checked_at`, `started_at`).
    Clock,
    /// An identifier the run drew, or a value derived from one (`tx`, `plan_id`).
    NewId,
    /// A value derived from file identity (inode, birth time, change time), which no copy of a home keeps.
    Identity,
}
impl VolatileKind {
    /// README's word: "timing", "wall clock", "new id", "file identity".
    pub const fn word(self) -> &'static str;
}

/// A key whose value may differ between two runs of one command on one unchanged home.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Volatile {
    /// `*` for every command, or one `app::COMMANDS` path.
    pub command: &'static str,
    /// A path from the envelope's root, in A9's syntax (`a.b`, `a[]`, `a{}`), plus `*` (any one key) and `**`
    /// (any run of keys and items, none included).
    pub path: &'static str,
    pub kind: VolatileKind,
    /// One sentence, ending with a period.
    pub reason: &'static str,
}

/// Sorted by (command, path), `*` first. The only list any comparison of outputs masks.
pub const VOLATILE: &[Volatile] = &[ … ];

/// One step of a concrete path into an envelope.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Step<'a> { Key(&'a str), Index }

/// Whether `pattern` matches `path`, anchored at both ends.
pub fn matches(pattern: &str, path: &[Step<'_>]) -> bool;

/// The row of `table` that declares the value at `path` of an envelope of `command` volatile, if any
/// (`declared` is `declared_in(VOLATILE, …)`; the table parameter exists for tests that drop rows).
pub fn declared_in(table: &'static [Volatile], command: &str, path: &[Step<'_>]) -> Option<&'static Volatile>;
pub fn declared(command: &str, path: &[Step<'_>]) -> Option<&'static Volatile>;
```

`src/contract/mod.rs` gains `pub use volatile::{Step, Volatile, VolatileKind, VOLATILE};` (and `pub mod volatile;` if it is not declared yet). The module doc says what the registry is, that the contract suite masks exactly these keys, that a later leaf adding a volatile key adds its row here and its line to README's table, and the four kinds.

Decided while writing this bead: the struct replaces the `(pattern, reason)` tuple, because the audit needs a command scope (`data.sha256` is volatile for `backup export` only, and must stay checked for `install`, whose blob hashes are content) and a kind (README explains each row by it). Paths are rooted at the envelope, not at a schema as `OPAQUE`'s are, because the repeat masks envelopes. A doctor envelope's report keys sit at its root (`run_id`); a general command's data sits under `data`.

### 2. The path grammar

A pattern splits on `.` into segments. Each segment is one of:

- `name`: the key `name`;
- `name[]`: the key `name`, then one array item;
- `name{}`: the key `name`, then any one key (a map's value);
- `*`: any one key;
- `**`: any run of steps (keys or items), none included.

A concrete path is a list of `Step::Key(name)` and `Step::Index`. `matches` is a small backtracking function over the two lists, no regex: `**` tries zero steps first, then one more each time. Patterns that do not parse (an empty segment, `[]` alone, `**[]`) are a programming error found by `volatile_rows_are_sorted_unique_and_reasoned`.

### 3. The rows after this part

Migrate every existing row, keeping its `path` and `reason` (reworded only to one sentence ending with a period): command `*` for the general and doctor rows, the documented command for F3's `age_s` rows (two rows, `doctor diagnose` and `doctor fix`, for `findings[].evidence.age_s`; one, `doctor explain`, for `instances[].evidence.age_s`), with kinds `Timing` for durations, `Clock` for instants and ages measured from now (`age_s` is a `Clock`: it is now minus a recorded time), and `NewId` for plan and run ids. Then add the three plan rows no leaf declared yet:

| command | path | kind | reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| `*` | `data.**.tx` | new id | A real run commits a new transaction. |
| `*` | `data.**.run` | new id | A real run over several clients, or a backup run, shares a new run id. |
| `*` | `data.**.checked_at` | wall clock | A run that asks a source records when it asked. |

The rows the repeat finds (backup ids, registered target keys, identity-bound tokens, the doctor's `tx` and identities) are `omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.8.3.2`'s to add, because only the repeat shows which are needed. Do not add them here.

### 4. The schema check

`every_volatile_row_names_a_schema_position`: for each row and each command in its scope (`*` means every `COMMANDS` row; the row must then match at least one), collect the envelope-rooted positions of that command and require the row's pattern to match at least one:

- For a `General`-dictionary command: every `Visit::Position` and `Visit::Property` path of `envelope_schema()`, except the `data` subtree, plus every path of `command_schema(entry)` prefixed with `data` (`targets[].tx` → `data.targets[].tx`; the root → `data`).
- For a `Doctor`-dictionary command: every path of `command_schema(entry)` (the report, at the root), plus the general envelope keys E3's doctor envelope carries (`ok`, `schema_version`, `tool_version`, `command`, `exit_code`, `error`, `warnings`, `commands`, `meta` and `meta`'s own keys). Use E3's doctor envelope schema directly when `contract::schema` has one.
- A schema path is turned into steps by the same grammar: `a.b` keys, `[]` an item, `{}` a key that matches any pattern key or `*`.

A row matching no position fails: "contract::VOLATILE row (`<command>`, `<path>`) names no position of its commands' schemas; fix the path or remove the row". Decided while writing this bead: this static check replaces an "every row was observed in the matrix" check. It catches a typo or a field removed since, and does not force a matrix row for every rare evidence key a doctor fixture produces.

### 5. README

After `### Fields` in "The JSON contract", add:

```markdown
### Volatile keys

Two runs of one command on one unchanged home print the same bytes, keys in the same order (deterministic, but member
order is not part of the contract: see Stability), except the values of the keys below. The contract suite masks exactly
these when it compares repeated runs, and a test holds this table equal to `contract::VOLATILE` (`src/contract/volatile.rs`).
Paths start at the envelope's root: `[]` is any array item, `*` any one key, `**` any run of keys and items. A masked
text value is masked too where it reappears inside another string: an `undo <tx>` suggestion carries the new `tx`.

| Command | Key | Kind | Why it differs |
|---|---|---|---|
| every command | `meta.*` | timing | The envelope's run facts: how long the run took, and whether it ran offline. |
```

One line per row, in the registry's order; "every command" stands for `*`; the command and key in backticks; the kind as `word()`; the reason verbatim. If E3 already added a note about the doctor's volatile keys under the JSON contract, replace it with a pointer to this table.

