## Technical Approach

Leaves named in this section: T1 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.9.1`, structured test logs, and the `argc e2e` runner), T3 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.9.3`, slice 2 end to end), A3 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.1.3`, schemas, declared `details`, and live validation), A4 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.1.4`, `omaddons capabilities`), A10 (`omad-m4-agent-surface-doctor-c6cm.1.10`, the semantic contract manifest).

### 1. Files

- `tests/e2e_slice1.rs` (new): the crate doc, `mod common;`, the two included contract files, `mod support`, `mod units`, `mod self_description`.
- `tests/contract/snapshot_set.rs` (new), `tests/contract/snapshot_walk.rs`, `tests/contract/main.rs`: only if section 3 requires the move.
- `README.md`: the suite's row in `### Tests`.

Nothing under `src/` changes; no dependency, environment variable, constant, snapshot, fixture or wire name is added; `tests/common/` is untouched.

### 2. The frame of `tests/e2e_slice1.rs`

The crate doc's first line is T1's marker; the rest says what the suite proves and how to run it:

```rust
//! e2e suite: slice 1's exit demo (self-describing), one scenario per demo item
//!
//! The M4 plan's slice 1 ends with this demo: `capabilities --json` round-trips;
//! `robot-docs schema` validates the whole matrix and every envelope snapshot, `details`
//! included; the manifest's report prints; `robot-docs guide` equals `SKILL.md`,
//! frontmatter first; `install-skill` into a throwaway `HOME`; `omaddons retail update`
//! suggests `update -t retail`, `remove dbm retail` changes nothing, and the corpus runs;
//! `completions bash` passes `bash -n`. Each item is a test below, built on the owning
//! leaf's helpers. Run with `argc e2e slice1`; logs land in
//! `target/tmp/test-logs/e2e_slice1/<test>/`.

mod common;

#[cfg(test)]
#[allow(dead_code)]
#[path = "contract/validate.rs"]
mod validate;

#[cfg(test)]
#[allow(dead_code)]
#[path = "contract/snapshot_set.rs"]
mod snapshot_set;
```

`#[cfg(test)]` makes clippy treat the included files as test code; `#[allow(dead_code)]` covers the parts this crate does not use (validate.rs's process-wide schema cache, for one).

**Decided while writing this bead: one file with inline modules** (`support`, `units`, `self_description` here; `guide_and_skill`, `completions` in part 2; `first_guess` in part 3), not `#[path]` module files, because T1's convention test checks that a file crate's root file calls `tlog!(`, and because T1's self-test crate uses the same pattern. Leave a short comment where each later module goes, so parts 2 and 3 append without reordering.

**`#[cfg(test)] mod support`** (each function documented; pure ones unit-tested in `units`):

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize)]
pub struct FoundTest { pub name: String, pub line: usize, pub ignored: bool }

/// Every `#[test]` function of `source`: a `fn <name>` line (after indentation and
/// optional `pub`, `async`) preceded by a block of attribute and comment lines that holds
/// `#[test]`; `ignored` when an attribute line of that block contains `ignore`. Any
/// other line ends the block. A line scanner, not a parser: it guards names.
pub fn scan_tests(source: &str) -> Vec<FoundTest>;
/// Whether `needle` occurs on a line whose trimmed start is not `//` (a needle inside a
/// string literal on a code line counts: an accepted limitation).
pub fn calls(source: &str, needle: &str) -> bool;
/// `source` without any whitespace, for token-sequence searches that survive rustfmt.
pub fn squeezed(source: &str) -> String;
/// Lines of `<state>/omaddons/net.log`; 0 when the file does not exist.
pub fn net_log_lines(state: &Path) -> usize;
/// `<CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR>/<rel>` as text; panics naming `rel` when unreadable.
pub fn repo_file(rel: &str) -> String;
/// Logs every test found in `rel`, then panics, naming each missing or ignored name,
/// the demo item that loses its evidence, and "restore the test, or point this guard at
/// its replacement", unless each of `names` is a `#[test]` of `rel` that is not ignored.
pub fn assert_tests_in_gate(log: &TestLog, rel: &str, names: &[&str], demo_item: &str);
/// `["omaddons", rest…]` → `[rest…, "--json", "--progress", "json"]`; panics on any
/// other first word.
pub fn fix_run_argv(fix_argv: &[String]) -> Vec<String>;
/// The `version` of addon `name` (case-insensitive) on target `id` in `list --json`'s
/// data; `None` when not managed there.
pub fn listed_version(list_data: &Value, id: &str, name: &str) -> Option<String>;

/// What `robot-docs schema --json` published, and strict validators compiled from it.
pub struct Published { pub raw: Value, pub frame: validate::Validator, pub commands: BTreeMap<String, validate::Validator> }
/// Runs `robot-docs schema --json` through the log (exit 0), checks the four keys,
/// logs the counts (never the whole listing), and compiles `frame` from `envelope` and
/// one validator per `commands` entry, all strict; a schema that does not compile
/// fails naming it.
pub fn published(log: &TestLog, env: &TestEnv) -> Published;
/// Every failure of one envelope snapshot: the whole envelope against `frame` (whose
/// `data` is open and whose `error` is the code-discriminated union, so `details` is
/// checked), then, unless frame-only, `data` against `commands[<command>]`; a command
/// the listing does not publish is a failure. One line each:
/// `<repo-relative file> <frame|data> <instance path>: <message> (schema <schema path>)`.
pub fn snapshot_failures(p: &Published, snap: &snapshot_set::EnvelopeSnapshot) -> Vec<String>;
```

`fix_run_argv` and `listed_version` are used by part 3; they live here with the other helpers and their unit tests, so part 3 needs no change to `support`. Decided while writing this bead: `net.log` is counted raw from the file, because T1's `Ran` records the lines a run added in the log but exposes no accessor for them in its listed API, and opening the library's `NetLog` from a test could create the directory it observes.

### 3. Sharing the snapshot walker's collection

A3's walker (`tests/contract/snapshot_walk.rs`, test `every_envelope_snapshot_validates`) reads every `.snap` file under `tests/` and `src/` (not `.snap.new`), takes the body after insta's second `---` line, keeps each that parses as a JSON object with `ok`, `schema_version` and `command`, substitutes typed samples for whole-value placeholders in values and keys (`<ulid>`, `<tx>`, `<key>`, `<run>` → 26 Crockford zeros, matching the ULID pattern; `<time>`, `<checked_at>` → `"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"`; `<bytes>` → integer `0`; placeholders inside longer strings and `<version>`, `<precondition>` stay; any other whole-value `<[a-z_]+>` fails naming the file and JSON path), inserts `"meta": {"elapsed_ms": 0, "offline": false}` when absent, and treats three renderer fixtures as `FRAME_ONLY` (`src/output/snapshots/omaddons__output__tests__envelope_success.snap`, `…_partial_success.snap`, `…_check_failed.snap`).

This suite must use exactly that collection, so the two can never disagree about what an envelope snapshot is. Decided while writing this bead:
- If A3 landed the collection and substitution in a file that holds no `#[test]` and depends only on `std`, `serde_json` (and `validate.rs`), include that file with `#[path]` (under its own module name instead of `snapshot_set`).
- Otherwise, **move** those items unchanged (no logic edit) from `snapshot_walk.rs` to a new `tests/contract/snapshot_set.rs`, add `mod snapshot_set;` to `tests/contract/main.rs`, and make `snapshot_walk.rs` call them there. The tests `every_envelope_snapshot_validates` and `placeholder_table_cases` stay in `snapshot_walk.rs` and run once, in the contract binary. Run `cargo test --locked --test contract every_envelope_snapshot_validates -- --nocapture` before and after: its summary line (`validated <n> envelope snapshots (<f> frame-only) of <m> .snap files`) must be identical; quote both in the close comment. Public items of the moved file, keeping the walker's names where they differ: `EnvelopeSnapshot { path: PathBuf /* repo-relative */, envelope: Value /* substituted, meta inserted */, frame_only: bool }`; `envelope_snapshots(repo: &Path) -> Result<(Vec<EnvelopeSnapshot>, usize /* .snap files scanned */), String>` (sorted by path; `Err` on an unknown placeholder); `substitute(&mut Value) -> Result<(), String>`; `FRAME_ONLY: &[(&str, &str)]` (path, reason). The file depends only on `std` and `serde_json`.
- Never copy the table into this suite.

### 4. `mod self_description`

Each test begins `let log = tlog!();`. Every command goes through the log; every state claim through `log.tree`/`log.assert_unchanged`, `log.assert_eq`, or a `log.json` of the compared values before the assertion. Every run on a home with a `WTF/` passes `--progress json` and calls `assert_private()`.

#### 4.1 `capabilities_round_trips_and_writes_nothing` (demo item 1, criterion 1)

Home: `Home::new(&["wow", "wow_classic"])`, then `Wtf::at(home.client("_retail_")).saved_variables("DBM-Core", 256).build()`.

1. `log.step("prepare")`: `log.run(&home, &["status", "--json", "--progress", "json"])` → `ok()`, `assert_private()` (creates `<state>/omaddons` and `<cache>/omaddons`, so the purity check compares directories that exist).
2. `log.step("hash")`: `log.tree` of `state` (`home.env.state()`), `cache`, `config`, `retail` (`home.client("_retail_")`), `classic` (`home.client("_classic_")`); `let lines = net_log_lines(&home.env.state())`, logged.
3. `log.step("fetch")`: `let data = capabilities::fetch(&log, &home)`; `log.json("digest", &capabilities::digest(&data))`.
4. `log.step("the six keys")`: `version`, `contract_version`, `features`, `commands`, `exit_codes`, `env_vars` each present and not null; `log.assert_eq("contract_version", &data["contract_version"], &json!(omaddons::output::SCHEMA_VERSION))`.
5. `log.step("round trip")`: `let failures = capabilities::round_trip(&data)`; `log.json("round-trip failures", &failures)`; `log.json("checked", {commands, flags, aliases})` counted from `data.commands`; assert empty, listing the failures.
6. `log.step("completeness")`: `capabilities::completeness(&data)` logged and empty; `log.assert_eq` of `commands` with `omaddons::app::COMMANDS.len()`, `error_codes` with `ErrorCode::ALL.len()`, `warning_codes` with `WarningCode::ALL.len()`, `actions` with `Action::ALL.len()`, `failure_modes.count` with `FAILURE_MODES.len()` (the transcript shows the numbers the demo comment quotes).
7. `log.step("the envelope")`: `log.run(&home, &["capabilities", "--json", "--progress", "json"])` → `ok()`, `assert_private()`; `schema_version` equal to `data.contract_version`; no stderr line with `"event":"error"`.
8. `log.step("nothing written, nothing asked")`: `log.assert_unchanged` for the five trees; `net_log_lines` equal to step 2's.

#### 4.2 `robot_docs_schema_validates_every_envelope_snapshot_details_included` (demo item 2, criterion 2)

Home: `TestEnv::new()`.

1. `log.step("fetch")`: `let p = support::published(&log, &env)`.
2. `log.step("every path, the envelope, every event")`: `p.raw.commands` keys equal `omaddons::app::COMMANDS`' names as a set; `p.raw.events` keys equal `EventName::ALL`'s wire names; `p.raw.envelope` is an object.
3. `log.step("published is what the contract suite validates with")`: `raw.commands == serde_json::to_value(contract::schema::command_schemas())`, `raw.envelope == to_value(envelope_schema())`, `raw.events == to_value(event_schemas())`, `raw.files == to_value(file_schemas())`; on a difference, log and fail listing every differing map key. Why this proves "the whole matrix": the contract binary's `support::check` validates every live matrix envelope (every success and failure row), every `error` against its code's variant and every stderr event, strictly, against `contract::schema`; with the published documents equal to those, every matrix envelope in the gate is validated against exactly what `robot-docs schema` publishes. Decided while writing this bead: equality plus the direct snapshot validation below, because a separate test crate cannot re-run the matrix.
4. `log.step("every envelope snapshot")`: `snapshot_set::envelope_snapshots(Path::new(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR")))` (an `Err` fails with its message); collect `snapshot_failures(&p, &snap)` over every snapshot.
5. `log.step("counts")`: `log.json("validated", {snap_files, envelopes, matrix, frame_only, with_details, failures: <first 50>})`, where `matrix` counts snapshots under `tests/contract/snapshots/` and `with_details` those whose `error.details` is a non-empty object. Assert no failure (the message lists them all), `envelopes >= 100` (123 at `c0ab5e9`), `matrix >= 100` (105), `with_details >= 30` (35). Decided while writing this bead: floors a little under today's counts, so a wrong root or a collector that drops files fails, while a leaf that rewrites a few snapshots never trips them.

#### 4.3 `published_schemas_reject_a_wrong_details_and_an_unknown_key` (criterion 2's strictness)

The negative controls: without them, a permissive schema would let 4.2 pass.

1. `log.step("fetch")`: `support::published(&log, &TestEnv::new())`; the snapshot set as in 4.2.
2. `log.step("pick")`, by content (decided while writing this bead, so a renamed snapshot does not break the test): `stale` = the first snapshot in path order with `error.code == "plan_stale"` and an array `error.details.changed` (`contract__matrix__if_match_stale.snap` at `c0ab5e9`); `success` = the first `ok: true` snapshot under `tests/contract/snapshots/` whose `command` is `pin`, `status` or `list` and whose `data` is an object (`contract__matrix__pin_ok.snap`). Log both file names; fail naming what was looked for if either is missing.
3. `log.step("the controls pass")`: both, unmutated, give no failure.
4. One step per mutation, each on a clone, logging the changed fragment and the failures:
   - `details.changed as a string`: `stale.error.details.changed = "targets"` → a frame failure whose instance path is `/error` or under it;
   - `an undeclared details key`: `stale.error.details.t2_planted = 1` → the same;
   - `an undeclared reason`: `stale.error.details.reason = "t2_not_a_reason"` (closed enum: `expired`, `binary_changed`, `input_missing`, `already_applied`) → the same;
   - `an unknown data key`: `success.data.t2_planted = true` → a data failure;
   - `an unknown frame key`: `success.t2_planted = true` at the envelope root → a frame failure.

#### 4.4 `the_manifest_report_prints` (demo item 3)

No command runs: the manifest has no command (M4-D56). The test logs through `TestLog` all the same, so the report sits in its transcript.

1. `log.step("build")`: `let (manifest, conflicts) = contract::manifest::build_checked(&contract::Registry::builtin())` (use the constructor name A4 and A10 landed if it differs); conflicts logged and empty.
2. `log.step("report")`: `let text = contract::manifest::report(&manifest, None)`; `log.note(&text)`; write it to `log.dir().join("manifest-report.txt")` (the only file a test of this suite writes outside a tempdir; replaced on rerun). `log.json("section_counts", &section_counts(&manifest))`. Assert: not empty; contains `contract::manifest::FORMAT` and the node total; for every `(section, count)`, some line holds both the section word and the count.
3. `log.step("against the empty baseline")`: `let changes = classify(&Manifest::empty(), &manifest)`; `let (additive, breaking, unclassified) = changes.counts()`, logged with `unsatisfied_kinds()`; assert `additive > 0`, `breaking == 0`, `unclassified == 0` (A10: against the empty baseline every node is new; an unclassified node is A10's defect).
4. `log.step("deterministic")`: a second `build(&Registry::builtin())`; both `render(&[])` byte-identical.

#### 4.5 `the_contract_suite_still_validates_every_matrix_envelope` (guard for "the whole matrix")

1. `log.step("the contract binary validates")`: in `repo_file("tests/contract/main.rs")`, `calls(…, "mod validate;")`, `calls(…, "validate_envelope(")` and `calls(…, "mod snapshot_walk;")`, each logged, all true.
2. `log.step("the walker runs")`: `assert_tests_in_gate(&log, "tests/contract/snapshot_walk.rs", &["every_envelope_snapshot_validates"], "robot-docs schema validates every envelope snapshot")`.
